{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-270976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that exceeds  $2\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,10,\\,15,\\,\\frac{45}{2},\\,\\frac{135}{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $2\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-270976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2000 \\\\ 10\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >2000 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >200 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(200) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(200) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(200)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(200)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(200)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈14.067.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $15$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2000.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-270976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-270976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-270976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that exceeds  $9\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n3,\\,6\\sqrt{7},\\,84,\\,168\\sqrt{7},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $9\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=3$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{6\\sqrt{7}}{3}=2\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $9\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >9\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1} & >9\\,000 \\\\ {(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1} & >3\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7})}+1≈5.805.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $6$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $9\\,000.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-277964" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277964" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that exceeds  $30$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,\\frac{8}{3},\\,\\frac{32}{9},\\,\\frac{128}{27},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $30$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{\\frac{8}{3}}{2}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $30.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >30 \\\\ 2\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >30 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >15 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(15) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(15) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1≈10.413.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $11$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $30.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-277935" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277935" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,8,\\,32,\\,128,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $58\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >58\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >58\\,000 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >29\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈8.412.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $58\\,000.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-277931" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277931" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-9743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n4,\\,28,\\,196,\\,1\\,372,\\,…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-9743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{28}{4}=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {7}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 4\\cdot {7}^{n-1} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {7}^{n-1} & >500\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({7}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(7) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1≈7.744\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-9743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-9743" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-9743" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $10$th term"] ["text" "the $6$th term"] ["text" "the $7$th term"] ["text" "the $8$th term"] ["text" "the $9$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-9743" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $8$th term"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-278053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that is less than $0.004$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n20,\\,4,\\,\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\frac{4}{25},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.004$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-278053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=20$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{4}{20}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.004.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.004 \\\\ 20{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <0.004 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.004}{20} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})}+1≈6.292.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.004.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-278053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-278053" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-278053" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,6$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,32,\\,16,\\,8,\\,…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{32}{64}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{6}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{7-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,6.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,6 \\\\ {2}^{7-n} & <0.000\\,6 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(2)}^{7-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6) \\\\ (7-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6) \\\\ 7-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-7 \\\\ n & >7-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n7-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}≈17.703\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $18$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-67493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $15$th term"] ["text" "the $16$th term"] ["text" "the $17$th term"] ["text" "the $18$th term"] ["text" "the $19$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $18$th term"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,31$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n125,\\,25,\\,5,\\,1,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.000\\,31$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=125$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{25}{125}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={5}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={5}^{4-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,31.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,31 \\\\ {5}^{4-n} & <0.000\\,31 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{4-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31) \\\\ (4-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31) \\\\ 4-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-4 \\\\ n & >4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}≈9.020.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,31.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-277971" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-277971" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-218498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,08$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n256,\\,64,\\,16,\\,4,\\,…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-218498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=256$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{64}{256}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =256\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{4}\\cdot {4}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={4}^{5-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,08.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ {4}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}≈11.805\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,08.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-218498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-218498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-218498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The $10$th term"] ["text" "The $11$th term"] ["text" "The $12$th term"] ["text" "The $8$th term"] ["text" "The $9$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-218498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The $12$th term"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that is less than $0.027$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n54,\\,36,\\,24,\\,16,\\,…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=54$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{36}{54}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =54\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.027.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.027 \\\\ 54\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <0.027 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.027}{54} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1≈19.746\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.027.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-67495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $20$th term"] ["text" "the $21$st term"] ["text" "the $22$nd term"] ["text" "the $23$rd term"] ["text" "the $24$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $20$th term"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The $2$nd term of a geometric sequence with a positive common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}$, and the $4$th term is $\\frac{2}{3}.$ Given that all terms of the sequence are positive, what is the first term of this sequence to be less than\n$0.000\\,11?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given that ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{2}{3}$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{4-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}}{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the common ratio is positive, we take the positive value. Now, since ${a}_{4}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and  $r=\\frac{2}{3}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,11.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n} & <0.000\\,11 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11) \\\\ (3-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11) \\\\ 3-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}-3 \\\\ n & >3-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n3-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}≈25.480\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $26$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,11.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-39656" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39656" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $24$th term"] ["text" "the $25$th term"] ["text" "the $26$th term"] ["text" "the $27$th term"] ["text" "the $28$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39656" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $26$th term"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $3$, and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$907?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=3$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 3 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{3}^{4}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{-4}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{n-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $907.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n-5} & >907 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(3)}^{n-5} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ (n-5)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ n-5 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5≈11.199\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $907.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-67516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $10$th term"] ["text" "the $11$th term"] ["text" "the $12$th term"] ["text" "the $13$th term"] ["text" "the $14$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $12$th term"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-278078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $972$, and the common ratio is $3.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $30\\,000$ is the{{field-1}}th term." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-278078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=972$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 972 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 972 & =243{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{972}{243} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $30\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >30\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >7\\,500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1≈9.122.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $30\\,000.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-278078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-278078" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-278078" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $5$, and the $6$th term is $40.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$2\\,020?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=5$ and ${a}_{6}=40$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=5$ and  $r=2$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{3-1} \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{n-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,020.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {2}^{n-3} & >2\\,020 \\\\ {2}^{n-3} & >404 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{2}^{n-3} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3≈11.658\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,020.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-39655" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39655" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $10$th term"] ["text" "the $11$th term"] ["text" "the $12$th term"] ["text" "the $13$th term"] ["text" "the $14$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-39655" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $12$th term"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $108$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,984?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=108$ and  $r=\\frac{3}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4-1} \\\\ 108 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\ 108 & =\\frac{27}{8}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,984.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >1\\,984 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >62 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈11.179\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,984.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce3c1f61bbf300e5f8abce5dc8081eef412cf85a1854617d535196af834826b"} {"question" "q-67515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $10$th term"] ["text" "the $11$th term"] ["text" "the $12$th term"] ["text" "the $13$th term"] ["text" "the $14$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "q-67515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $12$th term"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/state.json" "file_sha256" "a886a82feac5b266211a43933c9196e33cfe329c31098c3edcfac4c1c6c15a8f"} {"question" "e-4243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {32},\\: \\dfrac 1 4,\\: 2,\\: 16,\\: \\ldots\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/example-4243.json" "file_sha256" "74a74e518c271ffe8d63fe8c9620df80b3e4e9288a47ffb820efa79baebe06b5"} {"question" "e-4243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=\\dfrac{1}{32}$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {4}\\right)} {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {32} \\right)} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{-2}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{n-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{n-3} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {8}^{n-3} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({8}^{n-3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(8) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\, 000)} {\\log (8)} + 3 \\approx 9.643.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/example-4243.json" "file_sha256" "74a74e518c271ffe8d63fe8c9620df80b3e4e9288a47ffb820efa79baebe06b5"} {"question" "e-4244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n16,\\: 8,\\: 4,\\: 2,\\: \\ldots\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/example-4244.json" "file_sha256" "897e7bee32286e965b330d4ba9d19243fc898a6b75f8ce27ac29ba50999af7d5"} {"question" "e-4244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=16,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 8 {16}  = \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{5-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,1.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,000\\,1 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5 - \\dfrac { \\log (0.000\\, 000\\, 1)} {\\log (2)} \\approx 28.253.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $29$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/example-4244.json" "file_sha256" "897e7bee32286e965b330d4ba9d19243fc898a6b75f8ce27ac29ba50999af7d5"} {"question" "e-4245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $54,$ and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,000?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/example-4245.json" "file_sha256" "af98688b182cd7654c371bb359bb257fc53d3c3f9ab9230478ff5e77c0d9478c"} {"question" "e-4245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given that $a_{4}= 54$ and  $r= 3,$ we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for $a_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{a_n&=a_1\\cdot r^{n-1}\\[3pt]\n\t\ta_4&=a_1\\cdot 3^{4-1}\\[3pt]\n\t54&=a_1\\cdot 3^3\\[3pt]\n\t54&=27a_1\\[3pt]\n\ta_1&=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >1\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(3)^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (500)} {\\log (3)} + 1 \\approx 6.657.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060521/example-4245.json" "file_sha256" "af98688b182cd7654c371bb359bb257fc53d3c3f9ab9230478ff5e77c0d9478c"}] "usage" {"q-39655" [] "e-4245" [] "q-67515" [] "e-4244" [] "q-67493" [] "e-4243" []} "basis" 1944 "retractions" [[17592186092744 :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.0006$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$64, 32, 16, 8, …$"] [17592186092640 :question/problem "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $5$, and the $6$th term is $40$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n\n$2020$?"] [17592186092745 :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $108$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n\n$1984$?"] [17592186091967 :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n$\\dfrac 1 {32},\\: \\dfrac 1 4,\\: 2,\\: 16,\\: \\ldots$"] [17592186091967 :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is\n\n$$\na_1=\\dfrac{1}{32}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {4}\\right)} {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {32} \\right)} = 8\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{32} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{64} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{8^{2}} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 8^{-2} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 8^{n - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000$. So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 8^{n - 3} & > 2000000 \\\\ 8^{n - 3} & > 1000000 \\\\ \\log (8^{n - 3}) & > \\log (1000000) \\\\ (n - 3)\\log (8) & > \\log (1000000) \\\\ n - 3 & > (\\log (1000000))/(\\log (8)) \\\\ n & > (\\log (1000000))/(\\log (8)) + 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\, 000)} {\\log (8)} + 3 \\approx 9.643\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$."] [17592186091968 :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n$16,\\: 8,\\: 4,\\: 2,\\: \\ldots$"] [17592186091968 :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=16$, and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 8 {16} = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2^{4} \\cdot 2^{1 - n} \\\\\n&= 2^{5 - n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,1$. So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2^{5 - n} & < 0.0000001 \\\\ \\log (2^{5 - n}) & < \\log (0.0000001) \\\\ 5 - n & < (\\log (0.0000001))/(\\log (2)) \\\\ -n & < (\\log (0.0000001))/(\\log (2)) - 5 \\\\ n & > 5 - (\\log (0.0000001))/(\\log (2))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5 - \\dfrac {\\log (0.000\\, 000\\, 1)} {\\log (2)} \\approx 28.253\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $29$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$."] [17592186091969 :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $54$, and the common ratio is $3$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,000$?"] [17592186091969 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that $a_{4}= 54$ and $r= 3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for $a_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&=a_1\\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[3pt] a_4&=a_1\\cdot 3^{4-1} \\\\[3pt] 54&=a_1\\cdot 3^3 \\\\[3pt] 54&=27a_1 \\\\[3pt] a_1&=2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000$. So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1} & > 1000 \\\\ 3^{n - 1} & > 500 \\\\ \\log (3)^{n - 1} & > \\log (500) \\\\ (n - 1)\\log (3) & > \\log (500) \\\\ n - 1 & > (\\log (500))/(\\log (3)) \\\\ n & > (\\log (500))/(\\log (3)) + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (500)} {\\log (3)} + 1 \\approx 6.657\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186090306 17592186090307 17592186090308 17592186090309 17592186090310 17592186090930 17592186090931 17592186090932 17592186090933 17592186090934 17592186090936 17592186090937 17592186090938 17592186090939 17592186090940] "immutable_fields" [[17592186090305 :answer-field/correct] [17592186090305 :answer-field/id] [17592186090305 :answer-field/key] [17592186090305 :answer-field/type] [17592186090929 :answer-field/correct] [